     "answer": "<div><strong>SpaceBook learning answer</strong><ol><li data-example-step=\"0\" style=\"\">When hot, skin arterioles dilate, bringing more blood near the body surface.</li><li data-example-step=\"1\" style=\"display:none;\">This increases heat transfer from the skin to the surroundings.</li><li data-example-step=\"2\" style=\"display:none;\">When cold, skin arterioles constrict, reducing surface blood flow and conserving heat.</li></ol><button type=\"button\" data-revealed=\"1\" onclick=\"revealMathExampleStep(this)\">Next step</button> <button type=\"button\" onclick=\"resetMathExampleSteps(this)\">Start again</button></div><div data-example-answer style=\"display:none;margin-top:.7rem;\"><strong>Answer: </strong>Vasodilation increases heat loss, whereas vasoconstriction reduces heat loss.</div>",
     "plainAnswer": "Vasodilation increases heat loss, whereas vasoconstriction reduces heat loss.",
     "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning answer",
