     "qNo": "vii",
     "q": "Describe various type of kidney stones and their causes.",
     "steps": [
      "Calcium oxalate and calcium phosphate stones are the commonest types listed in the chapter. Increased urinary calcium or oxalate can favour their formation.",
      "Uric-acid stones involve uric acid; the chapter relates raised levels to gout and high intake of meat protein.",
      "Cystine stones contain the amino acid cystine.",
      "Struvite or infection stones are associated with urinary-tract infection.",
      "Reduced urine volume and excess stone-forming substances favour crystallization.",
      "The chapter also discusses hypercalcemia, hypercalciuria, hyperparathyroidism, hyperoxaluria and hyperuricemia as relevant conditions."
     ],
     "answer": "<div><strong>SpaceBook learning answer</strong><ol><li data-example-step=\"0\" style=\"\">Calcium oxalate and calcium phosphate stones are the commonest types listed in the chapter. Increased urinary calcium or oxalate can favour their formation.</li><li data-example-step=\"1\" style=\"display:none;\">Uric-acid stones involve uric acid; the chapter relates raised levels to gout and high intake of meat protein.</li><li data-example-step=\"2\" style=\"display:none;\">Cystine stones contain the amino acid cystine.</li><li data-example-step=\"3\" style=\"display:none;\">Struvite or infection stones are associated with urinary-tract infection.</li><li data-example-step=\"4\" style=\"display:none;\">Reduced urine volume and excess stone-forming substances favour crystallization.</li><li data-example-step=\"5\" style=\"display:none;\">The chapter also discusses hypercalcemia, hypercalciuria, hyperparathyroidism, hyperoxaluria and hyperuricemia as relevant conditions.</li></ol><button type=\"button\" data-revealed=\"1\" onclick=\"revealMathExampleStep(this)\">Next step</button> <button type=\"button\" onclick=\"resetMathExampleSteps(this)\">Start again</button></div><div data-example-answer style=\"display:none;margin-top:.7rem;\"><strong>Answer: </strong>Major types are calcium stones, uric-acid stones, cystine stones and infection-related struvite stones; concentrated urine and excess stone-forming solutes favour their development.</div>",
