   "activities": [
    {
     "title": "Identify the bones of the pelvic girdle, pectoral girdle, arms and leg by using the model of human skeleton.",
     "desc": "<p><strong>SpaceBook learning answer:</strong> On the model, identify the paired hip bones (ilium, ischium and pubis), scapulae and clavicles, humerus, radius, ulna, femur, tibia and fibula. Then locate wrist, palm and finger bones and ankle, foot and toe bones. Use the model’s labels to distinguish the girdles from the free limbs.</p>",
     "sourceReferences": [
      {
       "printedPage": 82
      }
     ],
     "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning answer"
    },
    {
     "title": "Compare the structure of skeleton, smooth and cardiac muscles with the help of prepared slides.",
     "desc": "<p><strong>SpaceBook learning answer:</strong> Record the actual slides: skeletal muscle has long parallel striated fibres and multiple peripheral nuclei; cardiac muscle has branching striated cells and intercalated discs; smooth muscle has spindle-shaped cells, a central nucleus and no striations. Do not invent features that are not visible at the available magnification.</p>",
     "sourceReferences": [
      {
       "printedPage": 82
      }
     ],
     "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning answer"
    },
    {
     "title": "Draw a diagram of sarcomere and label its parts.",
     "desc": "<p><strong>SpaceBook learning answer:</strong> Draw two Z lines, thin actin filaments attached to each, and central thick myosin filaments. Label the sarcomere, A band, I bands, H zone, M line and the overlap region. The relaxed/contracted schematic in long answer vi provides a starting layout.</p>",
     "sourceReferences": [
      {
       "printedPage": 82
      }
     ],
     "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning answer"
    },
    {
     "title": "Justify how the main functions of the skeleton are to act as a system of rods and levers, which are moved by the muscles.",
     "desc": "<p><strong>SpaceBook learning answer:</strong> Bones act as rigid levers, joints provide pivot points and contracting muscles apply pulling forces through their attachments. The resulting lever movement changes the position of body parts. The skeleton also supports and protects the body.</p>",
     "sourceReferences": [
      {
       "printedPage": 82
      }
     ],
     "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning answer"
    },
    {
     "title": "Justify why do the muscles pull but do not push.",
     "desc": "<p><strong>SpaceBook learning answer:</strong> Muscle contraction develops tension that pulls on attachments. Active shortening does not push those attachments apart. Opposing muscles or external forces produce the reverse movement.</p>",
     "sourceReferences": [
      {
       "printedPage": 82
      }
     ],
     "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning answer"
    },
    {
     "title": "Relate the bipedal posture of man with his skeleton and musculature.",
     "desc": "<p><strong>SpaceBook learning answer:</strong> The vertebral column and pelvis support an upright trunk, the lower limbs bear weight, and the feet provide a base of support. Coordinated trunk, hip and leg muscles maintain balance and produce walking movements.</p>",
     "sourceReferences": [
      {
       "printedPage": 83
      }
     ],
     "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning answer"
    },
    {
     "title": "Name the techniques for joint transplantation.",
     "desc": "<p><strong>SpaceBook learning answer:</strong> The intended term is joint replacement or arthroplasty: partial replacement replaces part of a damaged joint, while total replacement replaces the relevant joint surfaces with artificial components. These are prosthetic replacements, rather than routine transplantation of an entire donor joint.</p><p>Source: <a href=\"https://www.niams.nih.gov/health-topics/osteoarthritis/diagnosis-treatment-and-steps-to-take\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIAMS — osteoarthritis surgery</a>.</p>",
     "sourceReferences": [
      {
       "printedPage": 83
      }
     ],
     "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning answer"
    },
    {
     "title": "Justify the use of calcium in teenage and twenties can be a preventive action against osteoporosis.",
     "desc": "<p><strong>SpaceBook learning answer:</strong> Adequate calcium and vitamin D support development of peak bone mass during growth and early adulthood. Building stronger bones provides a better reserve against later bone loss. Nutrition is one factor alongside physical activity and other influences; the question does not imply that everyone needs supplements.</p><p>Source: <a href=\"https://www.niams.nih.gov/health-topics/osteoporosis/diagnosis-treatment-and-steps-to-take\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIAMS — osteoporosis and bone health</a>.</p>",
     "sourceReferences": [
      {
       "printedPage": 83
      }
     ],
     "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning answer"
    },
    {
     "title": "Reason out the rigor mortis.",
     "desc": "<p><strong>SpaceBook learning answer:</strong> After death, ATP production stops. Without fresh ATP, myosin cannot detach normally from actin, so cross-bridges remain attached and the muscles become stiff.</p><p>Source: <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/10-3-muscle-fiber-contraction-and-relaxation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenStax — muscle contraction and relaxation</a>.</p>",
     "sourceReferences": [
      {
       "printedPage": 83
      }
     ],
     "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning answer"
    },
    {
     "title": "Relate improper posture to bone/joint problems.",
     "desc": "<p><strong>SpaceBook learning answer:</strong> Posture changes how body weight and muscle forces are distributed. Sustained awkward positioning can increase strain on particular muscles, ligaments and joints. Balanced alignment and changing position help distribute loads; posture alone does not explain every skeletal disorder.</p>",
     "sourceReferences": [
      {
       "printedPage": 83
      }
     ],
     "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning answer"
    }
   ],
